{
	title: 'Emergency keys',
	description: 'Three keys always work, in every state: Ctrl-C quits, Ctrl-Z suspends, Ctrl-R restarts. They are found in raw input before any stateful decoding, so a half-read escape sequence, an unfinished paste or a busy editor cannot swallow them; the Kitty CSI-u forms count too. Handling is synchronous in the client and never waits on the host. Quit and restart restore the terminal and keep the last frame visible; restart exits with a dedicated code that ./run answers by starting again. Suspend restores the terminal and stops the process like any Unix program; on resume it re-enters raw mode and redraws. Record these as invariants in tasks/README.md, so no later feature may capture or delay them. Test with terminal fixtures, including keys split across or buried inside other sequences. Its cli.ts artifact has the old quit, suspend and restart handling.',
	status: 'done',
	needs: ['xk']
}
